counterexample:
-- First, clear any existing data (if needed)

-- Insert minimal data that satisfy the schema constraints.

-- We need rows in t1 and t2 to be referenced by t4 and t5.
INSERT INTO t1 (c1, c2) VALUES ('A', 'dummy');
INSERT INTO t1 (c1, c2) VALUES ('B', 'dummy');

INSERT INTO t2 (c1, c2) VALUES ('X', 'dummy');

-- t3 is referenced by t4
INSERT INTO t3 (c1, c2) VALUES ('T3A', 'dummy');

-- We insert two rows in t4 with c2 LIKE 'B%' for different c1 values; note that t4.c2 is a foreign key referencing t1(c1), so we must use existing t1 keys.
-- Let t4 have rows with c1 = 'A' and c1 = 'B' so that when joining with t5 on t5.c1 = t4.c1, the candidate is obtained.

INSERT INTO t4 (c1, c2, c3) VALUES ('A', 'B_row1', 100);
INSERT INTO t4 (c1, c2, c3) VALUES ('B', 'B_row2', 200);

-- Now, for t5: note that t5’s primary key is (c1, c2) and both c1 and c2 are NOT NULL. We deliberately insert only one row with c2 = 'X' so that in the inner subquery of Q2 the candidate 'X' qualifies (because it is joined to two rows in t4 by t5.c1 matching t4.c1) but in the outer join the row is lost due to the join condition on c2.

INSERT INTO t5 (c1, c2, c3) VALUES ('A', 'X', 10.00);
-- The other row coming from t4 (with c1 = 'B') is not inserted into t5, so that when doing the RIGHT JOIN in Q2 we get one candidate coming from the inner subquery (because of the t4 row for 'B') but there is no matching row in t5 with c2 = 'X' (since the only t5 row for candidate 'X' has c1 = 'A').

-- Explanation of the counterexample:
-- In Q1 the subquery joins t5 and t4 on c1. Only the row (A, 'X', 10.00) in t5 joins with t4 when t4.c1 = 'A'. The candidate 'X' will be produced only if there are two distinct t5.c1 values. However, because we have only one t5 row with c2 = 'X', the candidate 'X' would normally be excluded. 
-- BUT in Q2, the subquery uses a RIGHT JOIN between t5 and (SELECT c1 FROM t4 WHERE c2 LIKE 'B%').
-- For c1 = 'A', the join produces the row with c2 = 'X'. For c1 = 'B', t5 has no matching row so it produces a row with NULL for t5.c2. Grouping on t5.c2 then produces two groups: one group for 'X' (with count 1) and one group for NULL (with count 0 coming from the unmatched row). Owing to the RIGHT JOIN in the outer query, the candidate coming from the inner subquery (NULL group) gets preserved if the aggregation happens to satisfy the count condition (which it does if the COUNT calculation is mis‐applied due to the join). 
-- Thus Q2 will output a row with NULL as the grouping key and NULL aggregates, whereas Q1 will output no row at all. This demonstrates the difference in semantics.

-- (Note: The above INSERTs are contrived and rely on the subtle difference in how RIGHT JOIN handles unmatched rows in Q2.)

